% =======================================================
\section{Engineering Adaptation Pipeline}
\label{sec:adaptation}
\label{sec:adapt-impl}

The deployed tuner estimates wave time scale and acceleration level from a
measurement-only proxy and applies bounded smoothing and cadence rules to
$\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, and $r_S$.  No MEKF displacement, velocity, covariance,
or attitude state is fed back into the tuner.

\subsection{Measurement-only vertical acceleration}
\label{sec:vertical-proxy}

Wave statistics require levelled vertical acceleration, but using MEKF attitude
would make the schedule state dependent.  The implementation therefore uses a
private Mahony complementary observer
\cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary} driven only by raw gyro and
accelerometer measurements,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\vct\varepsilon&=\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}\times\hat{\vct d}(\hat q),
\qquad
\hat{\vct d}_{\mathrm{meas}}=-\vct f^{B}/\lVert\vct f^{B}\rVert,\\
\dot{\hat q}&=\tfrac12\,\hat q\otimes
\bigl(\vct\omega^{B}+\vct\beta+k_P\vct\varepsilon\bigr),
\qquad
\dot{\vct\beta}=k_I\vct\varepsilon .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:proxy-mahony}
\end{equation}
The resulting up-positive acceleration is
\begin{equation}
a^{P}_{\uparrow,k}
=-\!\left(\left[\mat{R}_{NB}(\hat q_k)\vct f^{B}_k\right]_{D}+g\right).
\label{eq:proxy-vertical}
\end{equation}
Only proxy tilt is used; yaw is irrelevant to the vertical channel.  The
default gains are $k_P=\SI{0.1}{\per\second}$ and
$k_I=\SI{0.01}{\per\second\squared}$ (the source stores $2k_P,2k_I$).  Its
correction corner lies below the wave band so gyro propagation carries
wave-frequency attitude while accelerometer feedback trims slow tilt and the
integral term removes standing gyro-bias tilt.  The proxy also supplies startup
attitude in Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}.

MEKF-based levelling would close a loop from attitude error through the tuner;
unlevelled body-$Z$ remains exogenous but suffers strongly integrated gravity
leakage.  The deployed proxy provides levelling with the one-way information
flow required by Sec.~\ref{app:iss-tuner}; both alternatives remain available
for ablation.

\subsection{Wave-period estimate}
\label{sec:wave-band-period}

The wave-direction acceleration carrier is not the tuning frequency.  Because
acceleration weights elevation spectra by $\omega^4$, that carrier remains near
the upper energetic band as sea period grows.  The tuner instead estimates the
elevation zero-crossing period $T_z=2\pi\sqrt{m_0/m_2}$.

The levelled acceleration $a^{P}_{\uparrow}$ passes through two high-pass stages
and two leaky integrators with common corner $\lambda$, producing velocity and
elevation proxies $v_p$ and $\eta_p$.  Their common filtering cancels in the
ratio, so for a narrow band
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sigma_{v_p}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}}=\sqrt{\omega^{2}+\lambda^{2}},
\qquad
\widehat{\omega}^{2}
=\frac{\sigma_{v_p}^{2}}{\sigma_{\eta_p}^{2}}-\lambda^{2},
\qquad
\widehat{T}_z=\frac{2\pi}{\widehat{\omega}} .
\label{eq:wave-band-period}
\end{equation}
The high-pass stages suppress very-low-frequency strapdown leakage without
changing this ratio; no MEKF position or velocity is used.

Once $\widehat T_z$ is valid, a fixed-horizon EMA forms
\begin{equation}
f_{\mathrm{tune}}
=\clip\!\left(\overline{1/\widehat{T}_z},
 f_{\mathrm{tune,min}},f_{\mathrm{tune,max}}\right).
\label{eq:tuning-frequency}
\end{equation}
Before then, a bounded wave-band prior is used.  The deployed smoothing time is
\SI{1}{s}.  This frequency sets the OU time scale, sigma-band corners, and
variance horizon; the separate acceleration carrier is retained only for wave
direction.

\subsection{Acceleration-scale estimate and averaging}

The same measurement-only vertical channel provides the stationary OU
acceleration scale.  Before variance estimation, the signal passes through a
period-scaled wave band with corners fixed relative to $f_{\mathrm{tune}}$.
The averaging horizon spans a fixed number of estimated periods,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
T_{\mathrm{eff}}&=\frac{1}
 {\clip(f_{\mathrm{tune}},f_{\mathrm{var,min}},f_{\mathrm{var,max}})},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{var}}&=\clip\!\left(K_{\mathrm{periods}}T_{\mathrm{eff}},
 \tau_{\mathrm{var,min}},\tau_{\mathrm{var,max}}\right),\\
\alpha_{\mathrm{var}}&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{var}}}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sigma-variance-horizon}
\end{equation}
The implementation uses $K_{\mathrm{periods}}=2$ with
$\tau_{\mathrm{var}}\in[\SI{0.3}{s},\SI{60}{s}]$.  A two-stage EWMA maintains
the band-limited second moment, and the band-adjusted sensor floor is removed
before square rooting,
\begin{equation}
\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2
=\max\!\left(0,
 \widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{tot}}^2-\sigma_{\mathrm{nf,band}}^2\right).
\label{eq:sigma-noise-subtraction}
\end{equation}
Thus the averaging interval grows with sea period while representing
approximately the same number of wave cycles.

\subsection{Targets, smoothing, and application cadence}

The engineering targets are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_\star&=c_\tau\frac{\widehat T_z}{2},\\
\sigma_{aw,\star}&=c_\sigma
 \sqrt{\max(\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2,\epsilon)},\\
r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{base}}&=c_R\sigma_{aw,\star}\tau_\star^3 .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:tuner-targets}
\end{equation}
The deployed coefficients $(c_\tau,c_\sigma,c_R)=(1.0,0.9,0.35)$ are empirical
calibration constants; Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-motivation} motivates the cubic
base scale but does not determine a complete-MEKF optimum.

Applied $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ use a common EMA,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}},\\
\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha(\tau_\star-\tau_{\mathrm{appl}}),\\
\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}&\leftarrow\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}
 +\alpha(\sigma_{aw,\star}-\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-ewma}
\end{equation}
with $\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}=\SI{1.8}{s}$.  The regularizer uses the slower
sea-scaled horizon
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_{r_S}&=m_{r_S}\tau_\star,\qquad
\alpha_{r_S}=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{r_S}},\\
r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}&\leftarrow
 r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}
 +\alpha_{r_S}(r_{S,\star}^{\mathrm{base}}
              -r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-RS}
\end{equation}
where $m_{r_S}=5$.  Candidates are committed every \SI{0.1}{s} and staged by
one IMU sample, so parameters used at step $k$ are measurable with respect to
data through $k-1$.

Pseudo-update cadence follows the applied OU time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\qquad
c_T=\frac{\SI{15}{ms}}{\SI{1.1}{s}},
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}
\end{equation}
with $T_{S,\min}=\SI{5}{ms}$ and $T_{S,\max}=\SI{250}{ms}$.  Cadence
renormalization preserves the historical information rate,
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}^{\mathrm{base}}
 \sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}},
\qquad T_{S,0}=\SI{15}{ms}.
\label{eq:engineering-rs-filter}
\end{equation}
Away from clamps, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:tuner-targets},
\eqref{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence}, and
\eqref{eq:engineering-rs-filter} yield
$r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}\propto\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}$; the cubic expression is
the base target before cadence renormalization.

\subsection{Three-axis application}

The current OU--III operating point is isotropic in stationary OU scale,
\[
(\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}})^{1/2}
=\diag(\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}},
       \sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}},
       \sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}),
\]
and integral pseudo-measurement standard deviation,
\begin{equation}
\vct r_S=
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}&r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}&r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
\end{bmatrix}^{\top},
\qquad
\mat R_S=\diag(\vct r_S\odot\vct r_S).
\label{eq:fus-rs-anisotropy}
\end{equation}
Paired results support this isotropic regularization over the tested directional
records.  Universal separate $x$ and $y$ factors are not inferred because surge
and sway are heading-dependent projections of the same directional motion.
The larger horizontal disturbance is addressed in
Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits} and the 3-D results rather than by a
heading-independent multiplier.

